  "query_preview": "../ Thursday through Monday /...Low pressure will \ncontinue to remain over the gulf before diving to the south \nThursday morning. As this low dives south along the coast towards\nHaida Gwaii, high pressure will start to build into the Yukon and\nBritish Columbia from the Alaskan Interior. With the ridge on the\nheels of the low, there looks to be a tight pressure gradient \nforming Thursday night into Friday morning. The orientation of \nthis gradient looks to favor north-south oriented water ways at \nthis time so we could see some significant northerly winds for the\nend of the week and into the start of the weekend. With the flow \nbecoming offshore during this time, we should dry out again thanks\nto drier air moving in from Canada. Some forecast guidance is \nalso leaning into 850mb temperatures dropping down to 0C as this \nair moves along the Coast Mountains but agreement on this is still\non the lower end. With the offshore winds and clearing skies, \ndaytime temperatures look to get back into the 60s and potentially\nsome 70s for some locations.\n\nHeading towards the start of next week, GEFS and some members of the \nEPS want to develop a low over the Northern Gulf but agreement on \nthis idea is still low. Otherwise, high pressure looks to remain in \nthe area heading into next week allowing for a fairly quiet pattern \nto set up for the area.",
